        "# Where You Grow Up Matters: Predicting Adult Income for Low-Income Children in the DC Region\n",
        "## Capstone Two: Modeling\n",
        "\n",
        "**Project question:** Which neighborhood characteristics best predict how much children from low-income families earn as adults, across census tracts in the DC metro area?\n",
        "\n",
        "**Goal of this notebook:** build and compare several models on the training data, choose the best one, and check it once on the held-out test data. It starts from the files saved in the Pre-processing notebook (`data/processed/X_train.csv`, `X_test.csv`, `y_train.csv`, `y_test.csv`).\n",
        "\n",
        "**Models compared**\n",
        "1. **Baseline:** always predict the average income rank (a yardstick, not a real model)\n",
        "2. **Linear regression:** simple and readable\n",
        "3. **Ridge and Lasso regression:** linear models that handle overlapping predictors\n",
        "4. **Random forest:** a flexible model that can capture curves and interactions\n",
        "\n",
        "**How the best model is chosen:** models are compared with cross-validation on the **training data only**. I then pick the **simplest model whose score is within one standard error of the best one**, because the most powerful model is not always the best choice. The test set is used once, at the end, as an honest final check.\n",
        "\n",
        "**How to use this notebook**\n",
        "1. In Colab, upload the four data files (or run the upload cell in Section 1).\n",
        "2. Run the cells from top to bottom. The random forest search takes about a minute.\n",
        "3. The cells labeled **Write-up note** are drafted in first person with the numbers this notebook produces. Read each one, confirm the numbers match your own output, and rewrite it in your own words.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Rubric check:** two to three different modeling methods applied and evaluated with appropriate metrics; shows when to apply which model and identifies one as best; a working, commented notebook uploaded in full to GitHub."
      ]
    },
