        "**Write-up note (guiding questions):**\n",
        "- **Does my data involve a time series or forecasting?** No. Each row is one census tract measured once, so the data is cross-sectional. Nothing needs to be predicted into the future, and there is no time order to protect, so a random train/test split (the one I made in the pre-processing step) is appropriate.\n",
        "- **Is my response continuous or categorical?** Continuous. The income rank takes 588 distinct values across 588 training tracts and runs between 0.29 and 0.68. That makes this a **regression** problem, not classification. Accuracy, precision, recall, and F1 are classification metrics, so I use **R-squared, MAE, and RMSE** instead.\n",
        "- The predictors were already standardized in the previous step, so I can fit linear and penalized models directly. A random forest does not need scaling but is not hurt by it."
      ]
