        "---\n",
        "## 3. Model 1: Linear Regression\n",
        "**Goal:** fit the simplest model that can show how each predictor relates to the outcome."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "6W04aysnen-K"
      },
      "source": [
        "**Write-up note (when to use linear regression):**\n",
        "Linear regression fits a straight-line relationship between the predictors and the outcome. I use it first because my outcome is continuous, my EDA showed mostly straight-line relationships (strong Pearson correlations that matched the Spearman ones), and it is easy to explain: each coefficient says how much the income rank changes when that predictor goes up by one standard deviation, holding the others fixed. Because the predictors are standardized, the coefficients are directly comparable. Its weakness is that it cannot capture curves or interactions, and it can give unstable coefficients when predictors overlap, which the EDA step flagged."
      ]
