        "---\n",
        "## 5. Model 3: Random Forest\n",
        "**Goal:** fit a flexible model that can capture curves and interactions that linear models miss."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "HRhRG8pLen-K"
      },
      "source": [
        "**Write-up note (when to use a random forest):**\n",
        "A random forest averages many decision trees. It can capture curved relationships and interactions (for example, a predictor that matters only in some states) without me specifying them, and it does not need the predictors to be scaled. I use it to check whether a flexible model finds structure that the linear models cannot. The costs are that it is harder to explain than a regression, slower to train, and it can overfit if it is not tuned. I tune three settings with grid search and cross-validation: `max_depth` (how deep each tree can grow), `min_samples_leaf` (the minimum number of tracts in a leaf), and `max_features` (how many predictors each split may consider). Limiting depth and requiring larger leaves keeps the trees from memorizing the training tracts."
      ]
    },
