        "---\n",
        "## 6. Compare the Models and Choose the Best One\n",
        "**Goal:** pick the final model with a clear rule that uses the training data only."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "7T3nt2J2en-L"
      },
      "source": [
        "**Write-up note (how I choose):**\n",
        "The most powerful model is not always the best one to use. Other considerations include how easy it is to explain, how fast it is, and how much it costs to maintain. I use the **one-standard-error rule**: find the model with the best cross-validated R-squared, then choose the **simplest** model whose score is within one standard error of that best score. I rank simplicity as linear regression (simplest), then ridge and lasso, then random forest. If a simple model is statistically as good as a complex one, I prefer the simple one because it is easier to explain, cheaper to run, and safer to maintain."
      ]
    },
