        "**Write-up note (answering the project question):**\n",
        "Across the models, the same predictors keep coming up. The random forest relies most on share_black2010, share_asian2010, state_VA, and the linear model's largest coefficients are share_black2010 (-0.035), state_VA (+0.020), hhinc_mean2000 (+0.019). Neighborhoods with a larger share of Black residents tend to have lower income ranks for children from low-income families, and neighborhoods with higher past household income and a larger Asian share tend to have higher ranks. The model also finds a difference between Virginia and DC that the neighborhood characteristics do not fully explain.\n",
        "\n",
        "**These are associations, not causes.** The predictors describe neighborhoods at the tract level, and they reflect a long history of segregation and unequal investment. A strong race-share predictor says something about the structure of opportunity across places, not about the people who live there, and the model cannot tell me what would happen to a child's outcome if a neighborhood changed."
