      "source": [
        "**Write-up note (summary):**\n",
        "I compared a baseline and three families of models on 588 training tracts using nested 5-fold cross-validation: linear regression, ridge and lasso regression, and a tuned random forest. Because the response is continuous, I judged them with R-squared, MAE, and RMSE. All of the models beat the baseline by a wide margin, and the three linear models and the forest scored within about 0.012 R-squared of each other in cross-validation. Using the one-standard-error rule, I chose **Linear regression** as the final model: it scores within one standard error of the best model while being simpler and easier to explain. On the test set it reaches an R-squared of 0.833 and an MAE of about 2.8 percentile points of income rank.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Limitations to mention to my mentor:** the sample is small (735 tracts); the outcome estimates are noisier for tracts with few children (I kept the reliability weight aside and did not use it in these models); the outcomes are for children born in 1978-1983, while the predictors describe neighborhoods in 2000 to 2015; and the results are associations at the tract level, not cause and effect.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Possible next steps:** weight the models by the number of children per tract, test additional predictors, or compare outcomes for specific groups."
      ]
